> Lets understand the different between Static or Dynamic Threshold:

# Static Threshold

You manually set a fixed value as the threshold. The alert is triggered when the metric crosses that specific value.

Example:
If CPU usage > 90%, trigger an alert.

Use Case of Static Threshold:

When you know what “normal” and “abnormal” values are.

Ideal for predictable workloads or known performance baselines.


# Dynamic Threshold

Azure Monitor uses machine learning to automatically learn the normal behavior of a metric and sets thresholds based on historical patterns.

Example:
Azure Monitor learns that CPU usually stays around 50–70%, so if it suddenly spikes to 90%, it triggers an alert — even if no fixed threshold is set.

Use Case of Dynamic Threshold:

For workloads with variable or unpredictable patterns.

When it's difficult to define a “normal” threshold manually.

