# Let's first see how to view, query, and analyze logs using Log Analytics Workspace:

Go to your Log Analytics Workspace.

Log Analytics will open with an empty query window, and the scope will be set to the Log Analytics Workspace itself by default.

If you're monitoring logs for a different service or resource (e.g., a VM), you can change the scope.

Click on the three dots (•••) in the top-right corner.

You will see that the scope is currently set to the Log Analytics Workspace.

Now, let's change the scope to our Virtual Machine.

Click on Change Scope.

Uncheck the currently selected Log Analytics Workspace.

Now, select the Resource Group, Resource Type (Virtual Machine), and Location.

Choose your VM and click on Apply.

Now the scope is set to your VM, and you can run queries and analyze logs collected specifically from that VM.

# Now let’s see how to do the same directly from the VM console:

Go to your Virtual Machine in the Azure portal.

Click on Logs under the Monitoring section.

This will open the Log Analytics query editor, but this time the scope is already set to the selected VM.

If you're only interested in logs from this VM, no need to change anything — all queries will return results only for that VM.

Again, you can confirm this by clicking on the three dots (•••) — you’ll see the scope is already set to your VM, so no need to change it.

In the empty query window, enter either Event or Syslog, depending on whether your Virtual Machine is running Windows or Linux.

In my case, I'm using a Linux VM, so I will type Syslog.

Then click on Run — the events collected within the selected time range will be displayed.
 
Now, you can see the system log here.

Note: If the query doesn't return any data, you might need to wait a few minutes until events are created on the virtual machine to be collected. 
You might also need to modify the data source in the DCR to include other categories of events.


