# Now, you question should be what is KQL?

So, Azure provides a powerful query language called Kusto Query Language (KQL). Using KQL, we can effectively query and analyze log data.

If you're already familiar with KQL (Kusto Query Language), you can type your query in the editor and click "Run" to see the logs based on your custom requirements.

If you're new to KQL, click on the three dots (•••) on the right side, then select "Learn KQL" to access Azure's built-in KQL learning resources.

# Using Default KQL Queries:

Azure also provides some default KQL queries you can use to quickly analyze logs.

To access them:

Click on the "Query Hub" on the right.

From here, you can select and run predefined queries for various metrics.

For example, to see logs related to Virtual Machine Available Memory:

Select the appropriate query.

Click "Run".

Choose your desired time range from the dropdown (you can also select a custom date).

The logs will be displayed below.

Example: Viewing Free Disk Space Logs

If you want to view logs for Virtual Machine Free Disk Space:

Go to the Queries Hub again.

Click Run.

Switch to KQL Mode (if not already selected).

Run the query to view the logs.

Note: If the log data doesn’t appear immediately, please wait for some time.

Sharing, Saving, and Creating Dashboards

Click on "Share", and you'll see several options:

Share the log report.

Save the report.

Pin the report to an Azure Dashboard for continuous monitoring.

To create a dashboard:

Click "Save".

Select "Pin to Azure Dashboard".

Choose Create New.

Set the type as either Shared or Private.

Enter a name for the dashboard and click "Create and Pin".

To view your dashboard:

Click on the menu (three lines).

Go to "Dashboard".

Expand "My Dashboard" and select your dashboard.

From here, you can monitor resource metrics in real time, download the dashboard, edit it, or share it with others.

